%S A398886 3,5,7,101,103,107,109,113,131,139,151,167,179,181,379,389,421,433,
%T A398886 439,449,463,467,491,547,563,941,947
%N A398886 Primes where the set of jumping champions changes.
%C A398886 A number is a jumping champion at p if no value occurs more frequently as the difference of consecutive primes <= p.
%C A398886 3 together with primes prime(k) >= 5 such that A087102(k) != A087102(k-1).
%C A398886 a(28) is expected to be around 1.7427*10^35; see Odlyzko, Rubinstein, and Wolf (1999).
%H A398886 Andrew Odlyzko, Michael Rubinstein, and Marek Wolf, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/10586458.1999.10504393">Jumping Champions</a>, Experimental Mathematics 8(2) (1999), 107-118; alternative links: <a href="https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~odlyzko/doc/jumping.champions.pdf">University of Minnesota</a>, <a href="https://projecteuclid.org/journals/experimental-mathematics/volume-8/issue-2/Jumping-champions/em/1047477055.full">Project Euclid</a>. See Table 1.
%H A398886 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/JumpingChampion.html">Jumping Champion</a>.
